>> title: Taro Cake
>> category: Flatbreads & Pancakes
>> tags: taro, rice flour, chinese, steamed, pan-fried
>> counters: Dim Sum Counter
>> aka: wu tau go, wu tao gou, yu tou gao, 芋頭糕, taro cake, fried taro cake
>> servings: 12
>> time: 14 hr
>> step.1: fry the shrimp, bacon and mushroom 5 min
>> step.2: toss the taro cubes in the seasoning 4 min
>> step.3: stir the hot stock into the slurry, fold the taro through
>> step.4: steam 50 min, cool 1 hr, chill 12 hr
>> step.5: pan-fry the slices 8 min, turning once

Fry @dried shrimp{1/4%cup}(25 g; soaked 20 min and chopped), @Chinese cured pork belly{3%oz}(85 g; lap yuk, finely diced) and @dried shiitake mushrooms{4}(soaked and diced) in @peanut oil{1%Tbs} in a #wok{} for ~fry{5%min}.

Toss @taro{1 1/2%lb}(680 g; peeled and cut into 1/2-in cubes) through @&(~1)savouries{} with @kosher salt{1%tsp}, @sugar{1%tsp}, @Chinese five-spice powder{1/2%tsp} and @white pepper{1/2%tsp} for ~fry{4%min}, just until the edges of the cubes turn. Taro is fried rather than simmered here — cubes that stay whole are what a slice of this is judged on.

Stir @hot chicken stock{1 1/2%cups}(360 mL) into @rice flour{1 1/2%cups}(210 g) slaked smooth with @water{1/2%cup}(120 mL), then fold @&(~1)taro{} through.

Steam @&(~1)batter{} in an oiled 8-in #cake pan{} in a #steamer{} for ~steam{50%min}, until a skewer comes out clean, then ~cool{1%hr} and ~chill{12%hr} overnight. Warm, the loaf will not slice; cold, it cuts like cheese.

Slice @&(~1)set loaf{} into 1/2-in slabs and pan-fry them in @peanut oil{2%Tbs} in a #skillet{} for ~fry{8%min}, turning once, until both faces are crusted and the taro inside has gone floury and sweet.
